00:00:40.910 --> 00:00:41.411
Source.

00:00:41.411 --> 00:00:42.410
ANITA KIBUNGUCHY: Great.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:43.430
Thanks, Craig.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:46.070
You can ask questions
anytime on the platform

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.810
and we have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.720
So, let's get started.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.940
So quickly on our agenda
today, the first thing

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.400
we'll start looking at is
the data analytics landscape.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.170
Next, I will introduce the
public and commercial data set

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:03.450
programs, and then
next, I will give it

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.710
to Craig, who will talk about
the GCP data provider program

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:09.420
and then talk about
Weather Source.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.290
He'll also go through a demo.

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:17.600
And then finally, we'll open
it up to a live audience Q&amp;A.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.229
So let's say I'm a
pharmaceutical company

00:01:20.229 --> 00:01:22.645
and I'm actually thinking of
building some smart inhalers.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:26.680
And the idea behind
this is that I

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.874
want to predict when
asthma triggers occur.

00:01:29.874 --> 00:01:32.290
And the first thing I'm sure
you will start thinking about

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:35.200
is that these smart
inhalers definitely

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.622
need to have some
sensors embedded on them.

00:01:38.622 --> 00:01:40.830
And part of these sensors,
as you can think about it,

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.260
will start collecting
a lot of patient data.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.680
They'll be collecting a lot
of like frequency of attacks,

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.220
how much usage is being
done-- so medication

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.245
usage, patient vitals.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:53.940
If you could think
about it, too,

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.300
if we were to do some type
of predictive analytics,

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.120
then we'll need some
form of weather data.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.590
If we're looking at temperature,
allergens in the air.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.870
And then of course, we'll
need some disease surveillance

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:10.370
data, region-specific
data, and then even just

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:12.870
a virus outbreak data.

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.400
The idea behind
this is that when

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.760
you're starting to think about
doing predictive analysis,

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.700
then you need all
types of different data

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.410
sets to be able to do
this type of analysis,

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.380
which makes a lot of sense.

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:28.210
So you will have
sources from internal,

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:32.407
which would be what's collected
by the smart inhalers, but then

00:02:32.407 --> 00:02:33.990
at the same time,
you will need to tap

00:02:33.990 --> 00:02:36.990
into all these other
plethora of data sets

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:42.270
that will be provided by
different sets of providers.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:46.230
So we'll take a quick step back
and we'll look at the landscape

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.650
right now.

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.340
There is a lot of data
being produced right now.

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.550
And, according to
IDC, there will be

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:57.570
163 zettabytes of data by 2025.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:00.070
For me, this is a lot of data.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:02.190
I can't even imagine.

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.200
We're looking at a lot
of unstructured data

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:08.610
being introduced, a lot of
selfies that you can think

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.680
about, lots of cat videos.

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.230
How do we start to analyze that
and start to make sense out

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.650
of all that information?

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:20.600
And then on top of that, just
the idea of data complexities.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.080
We're dealing with a lot of
legacy applications, data

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:28.370
silos, where finance has
their own different data sets,

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:31.131
and then you will have
supply chain having

00:03:31.131 --> 00:03:32.380
their own different data sets.

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.790
But we know that
we need to start

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:35.540
merging that data
so we can actually

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:40.910
start providing insights and
actually being more productive.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:44.270
Another thing is
regulatory environments.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:46.610
There's been a lot of
talk within the last,

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.760
I want to say, year
just about GDPR

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.010
and how does that
affect the data being

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.432
collected by a lot of the
different organizations.

00:03:54.432 --> 00:03:56.390
And then finally, just
when you think about it,

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:57.960
just the limited skills.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.850
There's a lot of demand
for people who can actually

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:03.920
start to be able to do
these type of analysis

00:04:03.920 --> 00:04:06.560
and we're just seeing a lot
of limited skill sets becoming

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:07.460
very hard to recruit.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.000
And what's even more
interesting here

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.490
is that, this is a survey
that was done by Forrester.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.130
This was actually done
just earlier this year.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:19.990
And we're noticing
that enterprises

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:22.690
are very interested in expanding
their ability to source

00:04:22.690 --> 00:04:23.980
external data.

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.540
I think enterprises
are realizing

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.980
the benefit of being able
to augment the existing data

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:31.880
sets with external data.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:34.150
And so they know
there's a lot of value

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:38.470
and how do we now start bringing
that data to these enterprises

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:44.015
so that they can actually
start to get more out of this?

00:04:44.015 --> 00:04:46.640
So now I'll introduce you to the
public and commercial data set

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:47.960
program.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.660
But again, as you can imagine,
we'll take a quick step back.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.300
On the left hand side, we're
looking at a traditional data

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.280
analytics platform.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.690
In that type of platform,
we're looking at organization

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:04.260
spending, a lot of time doing
monitoring, performance tuning,

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.700
making sure that their
systems are reliable,

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.290
resource provisioning.

00:05:09.290 --> 00:05:13.490
And then if you look at
one person doing all this,

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:17.000
then the amount of time left
to do analysis and insight

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.529
becomes so small.

00:05:18.529 --> 00:05:20.570
However, when you look at
on the right hand side,

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.670
you have the serverless
data analytics model.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.490
And this is part of the
Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.040
The idea here is
that we take away

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:32.112
all those different complexities
and we let your people focus

00:05:32.112 --> 00:05:33.570
on the things that
they're good at,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.090
which is analysis and insights.

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:40.950
And so, again, quick
step back, we'll

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:45.180
look at some of the
different products

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.060
that we're providing
as part of GCP.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:51.630
We're providing a complete
foundation for data lifecycle,

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.090
all the way from
the point you're

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:57.960
ingesting data with products
like Cloud Pub/Sub and data

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.620
transfer service, and all
the way to advanced analytics

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:05.760
with Cloud ML Engine and then
things like Google Data Studio.

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.130
And then of course, we're
also providing the ability

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:10.620
to be able to orchestrate
all these things with Cloud

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:13.230
Composer, which is a
product that we've just

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.880
recently launched.

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:18.000
So again, complete foundation
for data lifecycle.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.190
And the idea here
is that we would

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:24.060
like to have our customers
really focus again

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.635
on analysis and insights.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:32.490
It's almost like, let us do
all the hard work for you.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.130
So now we'll do a
quick introduction

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.200
on the public and
commercial data set program.

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:38.910
And the idea behind
these programs

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:41.220
is that we're looking
to provide access

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:44.610
to data sets hosted on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.670
And what makes these
two programs distinct

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:51.660
is that users,
customers, organizations

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.180
can access and analyze
structured and unstructured

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.710
data in the cloud without
a need to move data.

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:59.130
The idea here is that we've
already provided the data,

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.790
it's being hosted in
BigQuery, in this case,

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:05.760
and all you have to do
is pretty much come in

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.610
and access the data there.

00:07:07.610 --> 00:07:09.360
The other thing we're
providing is the use

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.280
of familiar data
warehouse and analytics

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.280
software, things that you're
very, very familiar with.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.900
Again, if you're a
BigQuery customer,

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.400
this should not be too
complicated for you.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.310
It should feel
very, very similar.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.860
And then finally, while
also providing a simple

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.707
and easy to use interface.

00:07:25.707 --> 00:07:27.790
Again, I've mentioned data
can be created directly

00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:30.419
from the BigQuery web UI.

00:07:30.419 --> 00:07:31.960
The idea here is we
want to make this

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:37.240
as easy as possible
to be able to have you

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:40.120
start to create insights and
really get a lot of value

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:41.068
from data sets.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:45.800
And so I'm sure you're
probably curious.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.750
Those were sort of
like the similarities

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.770
between the two programs.

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:52.251
Now, what are the differences?

00:07:52.251 --> 00:07:54.500
The first thing is that from
a public data set program

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:57.890
perspective, data is freely
hosted and accessible

00:07:57.890 --> 00:07:59.010
to customers in GCP.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:01.500
Again, we host the data for you.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.970
All you have to do
is just come to GCP

00:08:04.970 --> 00:08:06.560
and you're able to access it.

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:09.500
From a commercial
data set perspective,

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:14.180
data is hosted in GCP by data
providers, like Weather Source,

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:17.630
and standard GCP usage
charges may apply.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:21.020
The second difference is that
for a public data set program,

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.450
users can actually
freely query up

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:27.740
to a terabyte of data per
month directly from the web UI.

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:31.160
Again, here,
honestly, it's free.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.679
And then from a commercial
data set program,

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:35.390
users just have to
license the data set

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:36.348
from the data provider.

00:08:36.348 --> 00:08:38.960
So in this case, if you are
interested in accessing weather

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.789
data, then all you
would have to do

00:08:40.789 --> 00:08:46.310
is try to figure out how to
license that data, again,

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:47.636
from Weather Source.

00:08:47.636 --> 00:08:49.010
And once you get
access, then you

00:08:49.010 --> 00:08:53.690
would definitely just be able
to access that directly in BQ.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.175
And then finally,
from public data

00:08:55.175 --> 00:08:56.799
sets, what we're
seeing a lot of people

00:08:56.799 --> 00:08:59.210
do is that it
provides a playground

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:02.530
for those new to big
data and data analysis.

00:09:02.530 --> 00:09:05.870
Because again, the data is
public and it's also freely

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.560
hosted up to a terabyte
of data per month.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.780
On the commercial data set
side, what we're doing is

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.880
we're providing
commercial data which

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:16.070
has been curated and
vetted for analysis.

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.380
So in this case, Weather
Source had made sure

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:22.920
that this data is ready
to be consumed by you,

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:24.740
and so this creates
a lot of value

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:25.880
for a lot of our customers.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:32.530
And here are some examples
for the different types

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.810
of data sets we have posted.

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:37.190
On the left hand side,
this is a public data set,

00:09:37.190 --> 00:09:41.410
so you can access things like
GitHub data, Medicare data, Dow

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:42.490
Jones data.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:44.440
So it just depends on
what type of questions

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.100
you're looking to get answered.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:49.060
And then on the commercial data
set side, as you can imagine,

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:52.390
Weather Source, of,
course, Remine, Xignite.

00:09:52.390 --> 00:09:54.480
These are just a
few of the providers

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.050
that we have hosted right now.

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:01.580
And we're also seeing
customers getting answers

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.854
to a lot of important questions.

00:10:03.854 --> 00:10:05.270
So if you're looking
to understand

00:10:05.270 --> 00:10:07.490
how real estate affects
traffic and long term

00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:10.090
investments in
infrastructure, then you

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:12.439
will probably want to access
HouseCanary data, which

00:10:12.439 --> 00:10:14.480
you would be able to join
with your own data sets

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:16.354
to be able to answer
these type of questions.

00:10:19.530 --> 00:10:21.720
And then, I'm sure you're
probably wondering, how

00:10:21.720 --> 00:10:23.347
do you access these data sets?

00:10:23.347 --> 00:10:24.930
And I think the one
place I would like

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.540
to send you to is Launcher.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:30.186
So if you were just to go
to Google Cloud Launcher,

00:10:30.186 --> 00:10:32.060
you should be able to
access these data sets.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:36.307
And here's just a quick snapshot
of what that looks like.

00:10:36.307 --> 00:10:37.890
And then, of course,
if you're looking

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:41.280
to get a little bit more
information about the programs,

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:44.790
then you could just go
directly on our website

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.070
under commercial data sets
and Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:50.220
You can also access the
public data sets as well very

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:50.880
similarly.

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:52.486
So again, just go
ahead and google it.

00:10:52.486 --> 00:10:53.610
You should be able to find.

00:10:56.480 --> 00:10:59.320
All right, so now I'll
welcome Craig to talk to us

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:00.740
a little about Weather Source.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:03.200
CRAIG STELMACH:
Thank you, Anita.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:06.000
As I mentioned earlier,
my name is Craig Stelmach.

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:08.360
I'm senior vice president
of business development

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:09.930
at Weather Source.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.320
Weather Source is a
technology company.

00:11:12.320 --> 00:11:15.560
And since 2004, we've been
providing weather data

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:18.620
and weather-based solutions
to a wide range of businesses

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:20.750
in almost every industry.

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:22.970
It's interesting to note
that weather is actually

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:26.090
the largest external swing
factor in the United States

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:27.110
economics.

00:11:27.110 --> 00:11:30.380
And annually, it results
in over $500 billion

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:36.020
in economic loss and other
large, large influences

00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:37.040
on business.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:42.020
And in 2017, Hurricane Harvey,
Irma, and Maria actually

00:11:42.020 --> 00:11:45.380
resulted in over $200
billion in economic loss

00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:47.490
and there were
countless lives lost.

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.090
However, despite acknowledging
this and understanding

00:11:50.090 --> 00:11:52.460
these numbers and the
impact of weather,

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:54.560
it amazes me every
day when I talk

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.970
to large companies and small
and mid-sized businesses that

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:03.020
do not use weather as part of
their overall weather business

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:07.084
intelligence strategy, or don't
even have a weather strategy.

00:12:07.084 --> 00:12:08.750
And that's where
Weather Source comes in

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.150
and we're here to help.

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.670
Our weather data is
essentially statistically

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:17.750
consistent, homogeneous data
that is perfect for analytics.

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:19.670
And unlike other
data providers that

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:24.110
actually take airport station
data only and interpolate

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:27.300
or extend that data to
your location of interest,

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:29.115
Weather Source
approaches its data

00:12:29.115 --> 00:12:31.040
in a markedly different way.

00:12:31.040 --> 00:12:34.200
We actually start with
your location of interest

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:37.610
and then we stage up to 15
different inputs, including

